          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is a combination code, and which conditions in this reference are coded with one?",
          "answer": "A combination code is a single code that holds two conditions, or a condition and its complication, together. The reference explains that where one exists, it is used instead of separate codes. This is why diabetes codes form such a large family. (Source: *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*.)\n\nThe reference names these conditions as coded with combination codes:\n\n- **Type 2 diabetes (E11).** The E11 family includes complication subcategories. Diabetic chronic kidney disease gets a combination code plus a code for the stage of kidney disease. A diabetic foot ulcer gets a combination code plus a code for its site and depth. The reference also notes that the common code E11.9 is one of these, and that some of these codes still need a second code. (Source: *Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories*.)\n- **Hypertension (I10 family).** I10 is used only when hypertension stands alone. When organ involvement is documented, the combination codes are:\n  - I11 for hypertensive heart disease. A heart failure code from I50 is added if heart failure is present.\n  - I12 for hypertension with chronic kidney disease, plus a code for the kidney disease stage.\n  - I13 when both heart and kidney involvement are present.\n  - I15 for secondary hypertension, which is coded together with its underlying cause.\n  - I16 separates hypertensive urgency from hypertensive emergency.\n\n  (Source: *Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*.)\n\nThe reference also explains that when the index links two conditions with \"with,\" the classification presumes a relationship unless the record says the two are unrelated. (Source: *Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories*.)\n\nThe retrieved passages do not cover every condition that has a combination code, and they do not explain how coders choose between codes in every case. They are a summary for education only. Coding decisions are made from a full clinical record, usually by trained coders.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
